ring\". This implies the function should be called when `entries_b64` exists. If it doesn't exist, maybe that's also a problem? Or maybe the function should handle missing `entries_b64` by returning a problem string.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The reviewer found that \"an orphan explained only by its statement could hide an attested entry\". The fix is to require orphans to include the entries they're hiding (via `entries_b64`), and validate them. So:\n1. Add `entries_b64` to the allowed fields for orphan\n2. Make it required (or at least, validate it when present)\n3. Add the validation function\n4. Call it in `cmd_monitor`\n\nBut the existing test creates an orphan without `entries_b64` and expects it to pass. So I need to either:\n- Make `entries_b64` optional in the schema, and only validate when present\n- Or update the test (but the task says all existing tests must pass)\n\nGiven the constraint that existing tests must pass, I'll make `entries_b64` optional in the schema, and in `cmd_monitor`, if it's present, validate it. If it's not present, maybe that's also a problem? But the existing test would fail.\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\" This could mean: if an orphan has `entries_b64`, validate it; if it doesn't have `entries_b64`, that's also a problem (since it should have the hidden entries). But the existing test creates an orphan without `entries_b64` and expects success.\n\nHmm, maybe the test is old and the new requirement is that orphans MUST have `entries_b64`. But the task says \"All existing tests... must still pass.\" This is a contradiction unless the test is updated or the validation is only done when `entries_b64` is present.\n\nLet me look at the test again:\n```python\ndef test_orphan_entries_explain_attestations(self):\n    self.propose(\"a.json\", \"orphan\", {\"by\": \"owner\", \"log_index\": 12345, \"reason\": \"run failed after atte